sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch03-12> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Sequential substitution systems, which repeatedly apply the first matching search-and-replace rule like a text editor, give only simple behavior with two replacements and two elements, but with three replacements about one in 10,000 random rules produces seemingly random behavior.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Text-editor-like search-and-replace rules can generate great complexity";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 3, “The World of Simple Programs”; section “Sequential Substitution Systems” (from p. 88), p. 90. Scope: sequential substitution (string rewriting) systems"
}
